## Smart cast

**Smart cast** in Kotlin is a feature that will help us greatly reduce the code we place in the conversations we need. It is a way to automate or accelerate a cast safely. Something that Java has been criticized for for a long time is the verbosity it adds to its code. Sometimes it seems that we are saying something, and we must repeat it in the same sentence simply because the structure and semantics of Java require it.

Now, let's see how the smart cast, in conjunction with some rules, can verify what we need and allow us to use it if necessary.

Let's see an example:

```kotlin
fun testSmartCast(x:Any){
  if(x is String){
    println(x.length)
  }
  if(x is Int){
    println(x.inc())
  }
}
testSmartCast("Jose")
testSmartCast(9)
```

As we can see in the previous code, we have a function that we call testSmartCast. This function can receive x, which is of the Any type. Similar to Object in Java, Any is the subclass of all non-nullable types in Kotlin. This means every class and data type, except special types such as Unit, inherits from Any. So, we could be receiving different types of values. But within the function, what we do is have two conditions (we could do this in a better way, but for now, we want to make the code as clear as possible). We see that the first if condition prints the size of the string if x is of the String type:

```kotlin
if(x is String){
  println(x.length)
}
```

The second if condition increments the value of x if it is of the Int type:

```kotlin
if(x is Int){
  println(x.inc())
}
```

