# Lambda with a receiver

Now we are going to explore how to use a lambda with a receiver. To do this, we will revisit the code from the beginning of the chapter, where we worked with higher-order functions and lambdas. This time, we will focus on adapting the previously defined lambda to include a receiver.

In the initial code, we have this:

```kotlin
class MenuBuilder {
    private val items = mutableListOf<MenuItem>()
    fun item(name: String, action: () -> Unit) {
        items.add(MenuItem(name, action))
    }
    fun build(): List<MenuItem> {
        return items.toList()
    }
}
```

We don't need to make any changes to this code since we are only defining `MenuBuilder`. This is the code we mentioned:

```kotlin
data class MenuItem(val name: String, val action: () -> Unit)
```

The data class also remains the same, since it is not where we are going to have an impact either on lambda or with the receiver.

There is the `buildMenu` function implemented using only a lambda:

```kotlin
fun buildMenu(init: (MenuBuilder) -> Unit): List<MenuItem> {
    val builder = MenuBuilder()
    init(builder)
    return builder.build()
}
```

Now comes the part where we have to make changes to go from lambda to using lambda and a receiver:

```kotlin
fun buildMenu(init: MenuBuilder.() -> Unit): List<MenuItem> {
    val builder = MenuBuilder()
    init(builder)
    return builder.build()
}
```

232 Lambda

Modified buildMenu signature: We changed the type of the init lambda in buildMenu from (MenuBuilder) -> Unit to MenuBuilder.() -> Unit. Instead of passing builder as an argument, we call init directly on builder, allowing MenuBuilder to be the receiver within the lambda:

```
// Before
fun buildMenu(init: (MenuBuilder) -> Unit): List<MenuItem>
// After
fun buildMenu(init: MenuBuilder.() -> Unit): List<MenuItem>
```

As you can see, instead of passing builder as an argument to init, we now call init directly on builder:

```
// Before
init(builder)
// After
builder.init()
```

Inside main, we can now add menu items directly, without having to refer to builder explicitly:

```
val menu = buildMenu {
    item("Option 1") { println("Option 1") }
    item("Option 2") { println("Option 2") }
    item("Exit") { println("Exit") }
}
```

Since MenuBuilder is the receiver of the lambda, we can call item directly instead of builder.item. By removing the need to explicitly refer to builder, the code inside buildMenu is more readable and more concise.

We could use it, for example, for Domain-Specific Languages (DSLs). This structure is more friendly for the creation of DSLs, which is one of the main applications of receiver lambdas in Kotlin. This change makes the use of buildMenu more idiomatic in Kotlin and takes advantage of the capabilities of receiver lambdas to make the code more expressive and easier to read.

This code exemplifies three fundamental best practices in modern software design:

*   First, immutability is implemented intelligently. Although a mutable list is used internally to build the menu efficiently (MutableList), the external exposure is immutable, ensuring that once the menu is built, its state cannot be modified by external code.
*   Second, encapsulation is achieved by hiding implementation details through a private list and exposing only the necessary methods to interact with the menu, maintaining a clear and well-defined public API.

