# When to use generics

Let's explain it with code, for example, for flexible data structures:

```txt
// Instead of creating multiple classes:  
class StringBox(val value: String)  
class IntBox(val value: Int)  
class/personBox(val value: Person)  
// Create a single generic class:  
class Box<T>(val value: T) {  
    fun getValue(): T = value  
}  
// Use generic classes:  
val stringBox = Box("Hello")  
val,numberBox = Box(100)  
val personBox = Box(Person("Jose Lujan")) 
```

Instead of creating a separate class for each data type you need to handle, such as StringBox, IntBox, or/personBox, you can use a generic class that accepts a generic data type, T. This is accomplished by using the $\langle \mathsf{T} \rangle$ syntax in the class definition, which tells the compiler that T is a type parameter to be specified when instantiating the class. In this approach, each class is specific to one data type, resulting in code duplication and less flexibility.

If we think about the approach of using a generic class, it is much simpler.

Here, Box<T> is a generic class that accepts any type, T, allowing a single layout to handle different types of data. The getValue() method returns the stored value of the T type, which makes the class flexible and reusable.

Each Box object stores a specific type of data, but the code for the Box class remains the same. This improves reusability and simplifies maintenance, as you can work with any type of data without duplicating class logic.

Generics can also be used for utilities. Let's see an example:

```kotlin
// Generic function to process lists
fun <T> List<T>.processEach(action: (T) -> Unit) {
   forEach { item -> action(item) }
}
// Use with different types:
val numbers = listOf(33, 3, 38)
numbers.processEach { println("Number: $it") }
val names = listOf("Noemi", "Viena", "Jose")
names.processEach { println("Name: $it") } 
```

Let's go through the different elements of the code:

- <T>: This is a generic type parameter that represents any type of data. By using it, you tell the compiler that the function can work with a list of any type (List<T>), whether it is a list of integers, strings, objects, or so on.   
List<T>.processEach: This is an extension function that applies to the List<T> class, which means that you can use this function on any object of the List type.   
- action: (T) -> Unit: This is a parameter of the function that represents a lambda function that receives an element of the T type and does not return any value (Unit). This allows the behavior of processEach to be customizable.   
- forEach { item -> action(item)}: The function iterates through each element of the list usingforEach and executes the action passed as an argument on each item.

This example shows how generics and extension functions in Kotlin can make code more flexible and reusable, allowing you to apply operations to lists of any type easily and efficiently.

# When not to use generics

Generics are used when you need your class, function, or data structure to be flexible and able to handle different types of data. However, if you know in advance that your class or function will only work with a specific type, using generics can make your code more complex and less readable:

```txt
// BAD (overcomplicated):  
class UserRepository<T : User> { 
```

```kotlin
fun save(item: T)  
fun find(id: Int): T  
}  
// BETTER (simplen and clearer):  
class UserRepository {  
    fun save(user: User)  
    fun find(id: Int): User  
} 
```

In this example, UserRepository is defined as a generic class that will only accept types that inherit from User. However, if the UserRepository class is only going to work with the User class (and not with specific subclasses of User), the use of generics is unnecessary and complicates the declaration.

In the second definition of UserRepository, it is defined without generics and works directly with the User class, which makes the code clearer and easier to understand. The class fulfills its purpose without adding an unnecessary layer of complexity.

Another example of when not to use generics is when we have a simple action, such as just printing, and Any is sufficient. Let's see another example:

```kotlin
// If you only need to print items: // BAD (unnecessarily generic): fun <T> printItem(item: T) { println(item.toString()) } // BETTER (simpler): fun printItem(item: Any) { println(item.toString()) } 
```

Generics are useful when you want your function or class to be flexible and able to work with different data types in a safe and specific way. However, if your function doesn't benefit from that extra flexibility and only needs to handle things in a general way, using Any is a better choice.

In this example (first block of the code), the printItem function is defined with a generic parameter, T, which implies that it can accept any data type. However, if the function is just going to print the item by calling toString(), there is no need to use a generic type parameter, since all types in Kotlin inherit from Any, which already has the toString() method.

In the second part of the code, the printItem function receives a parameter of the Any type, which is the superclass of all types in Kotlin. This is sufficient because Any guarantees that the item has a toString() method, and there is no need to use generics if no specific operation is to be done based on the type.

# When to use Any instead of generics

You can use Any instead of generics in the following cases:

- When you don't need to do type-specific operations: If your function only requires using methods common to all types (such as toString(), Any is sufficient.   
- When you don't need type flexibility: If you're not going to perform operations that depend on the specific type of the element, there's no need to use generics.   
To simplify code: Using Any makes code clearer and easier to understand in these cases.

If your function only needs to work with elements in a generic way and does not need to do specific operations based on the type, it is better to use Any instead of generics. This simplifies the code and makes it more readable, avoiding unnecessary complexities.

