# What is asynchronous programming?

Now let's look at asynchronous programming. Unlike synchronous programming, we can have tasks that run independently and do not block the main flow of the program. We can say that, in this way of working, we can start a task, and since we do not know when it will end, we can send it to the background, while other tasks are also still running. This allows our program not to freeze and thus be able to continue moving forward or interacting with the user, or other tasks, without problems.

Let's go to the code. In this example, we will do the same thing we did in the synchronous example, but now asynchronously:

```kotlin
fun takeOrderAsync(callback: (String) -> Unit) {
    Thread {
        try {
            Thread.sleep(3000) // Simulates the order preparation time callback("Delicious Meal")
        } catch (e:遇到了Exception) {
            e.printStackTrace()
        }
    }.start()
}
fun main() {
    println("Taking the order....")
    takeOrderAsync { result -> println("Order served: $result")
} 
println("Serving drinks while waiting...") 
```

As we can see in this example, we are using asynchronous programming in Kotlin. The takeOrderAsync function uses an asynchronous operation with a separate thread, allowing the program to not block while waiting for the result.

The callback is invoked to print the result (Order served: Delicious Meal) when the job (preparing the order) is finished. Since the callback itself has a return type of Unit, it doesn't return any value to the caller; its only purpose here is to execute the printing.

This technique is useful when you want to handle tasks that take time without stopping the main execution of the program.

