Normally, we have to be careful with the order and type of values – that is, if we have four properties of the `String` type, then we will have to pass four parameters of the `String` type when creating the object. If the types do not match, then it is simply an error.

By recognizing that a constructor shares the same structure as a function, we can deduce that it can either be a constructor without parameters or one with parameters. Unlike Java, Kotlin does not support traditional constructor overloading. Instead, Kotlin allows the use of default parameter values in constructors, which makes multiple overloaded constructors unnecessary in most cases. However, secondary constructors can still be defined if a different initialization path is required.

