class StringBox(val value: String)
class IntBox(val value: Int)
class PersonBox(val value: Person)

// Create a single generic class:
class Box<T>(val value: T) {
    fun getValue(): T = value
}

// Use generic classes:
val stringBox = Box("Hello")
val numberBox = Box(100)
val personBox = Box(Person("Jose Lujan"))
```

Instead of creating a separate class for each data type you need to handle, such as StringBox, IntBox, or PersonBox, you can use a generic class that accepts a generic data type, T. This is accomplished by using the <T> syntax in the class definition, which tells the compiler that T is a type parameter to be specified when instantiating the class. In this approach, each class is specific to one data type, resulting in code duplication and less flexibility.

If we think about the approach of using a generic class, it is much simpler.

Here, Box<T> is a generic class that accepts any type, T, allowing a single layout to handle different types of data. The getValue() method returns the stored value of the T type, which makes the class flexible and reusable.

