In this code, we are using MockK to simulate the behavior of the Calculator class, instead of using a real instance. A mock of Calculator is created and configured with `every { calculator.multiply(2, 3) } returns 6`, which means that when the `multiply(2, 3)` method is called, it will return 6 without performing the actual multiplication. The test then verifies that the result is as expected (6) with `shouldBe 6`, and uses `verify(exactly = 1) { calculator.multiply(2, 3) }` to make sure that the method was called once. This approach is useful for isolating tests and avoiding dependencies on real implementations.

With this, we have reviewed the three most widely used options, although we could have mentioned other options. This, however, is a good starting point for exploring the world of testing. Other tools that could be used are Testcontainers and Spek. The former is used more for integration with databases and normally requires more resources than those already mentioned. The latter is more for BDD-type tests and is not yet widely adopted in the industry, but it is growing little by little.

